   'Scroll down to <strong>All conversions</strong> to see the same value in every unit at once, or press <strong>Reset</strong> to start over.'
  ],
  features:[
   'Seven categories with more than 60 units between them, including file sizes.',
   'Live results that update on every keystroke or selection.',
   'Correct formulas for Celsius, Fahrenheit and Kelvin, including a check for values below absolute zero.',
   'Both decimal (KB, MB, GB) and binary (KiB, MiB, GiB) data storage units, side by side.',
   'One-click swap, copy and reset buttons.',
   'A full comparison table showing all units for the current value.',
   'Handles very large and very small numbers with clean scientific notation.'
  ],
  uses:[
   'Converting recipe amounts between cups, milliliters and ounces.',
   'Changing kilometers to miles, or kilograms to pounds, when traveling or shopping abroad.',
   'Working out room and land sizes in square meters, square feet or acres.',
   'Translating oven temperatures between Celsius and Fahrenheit.',
   'Checking speed limits and vehicle specifications in km/h and mph.',
   'Working out how many megabytes are in a gigabyte, or converting a file size for an upload limit.'
  ],
  faqs:[
   ['How accurate are the conversions?','The tool uses internationally defined conversion factors, such as exactly 0.3048 meters per foot and 0.45359237 kilograms per pound, and shows up to ten significant digits.'],
   ['How is temperature converted?','Temperature uses formulas rather than a single factor. The tool first converts your value to Kelvin and then to the target scale, for example &deg;F = &deg;C &times; 9/5 + 32.'],
   ['Are the volume units US or UK measures?','Cups, pints, quarts, gallons, teaspoons, tablespoons and fluid ounces are US customary units. UK imperial gallons and pints are larger, so check which system your recipe or product uses.'],
   ['What is the difference between a megabyte and a mebibyte?','A megabyte (MB) is defined as exactly 1,000,000 bytes, the decimal definition used for file sizes and internet speeds. A mebibyte (MiB) is 1,048,576 bytes, the binary definition some operating systems use to report storage. This is why a drive labelled 500 GB can show as roughly 465 GiB in a computer&rsquo;s file manager.'],
   ['Why do I see numbers with an &ldquo;e&rdquo; in them?','Very large or very small results are shown in scientific notation, where 1.5e-7 means 0.00000015. This keeps the answer readable and exact.'],
   ['Does the converter need an internet connection?','Once the page has loaded, all conversions run locally in your browser, so they keep working even if your connection drops.']
  ]
 }
});
